The third of [[Andrew Blauvelt]]'s three waves: form, then content, then context. See [[Towards Relational Design]].

!! Where Blauvelt's context stops

For Blauvelt, context means people and what they do with a design: users, participants, networks, software that adapts. When he writes "ecologies" he means connected networks. His one material point is print-on-demand.

The [[Oka Chair]]'s context is a different list: fallen city trees, one farm's sheep, a mill, a makerspace. None of them is a user.

!! Where mine keeps going

//Yours to write.// Your 2021 note, [[Form <> Content <> Context]], already says context should decide content and form "for a particular time, space, culture." Is a bioregion one of those spaces?
